WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.218 --> 00:00:02.430
Narrator: In "X-Men: Days of Future Past,"

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.213
Evan Peters was filmed
running on a treadmill

00:00:05.213 --> 00:00:06.870
in front of a green screen

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.440
so he could then be placed
into these frozen shots,

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.610
which were filmed at over
3,000 frames per second.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.680
On set, these actors and objects

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.508
were stabilized with wires and poles,

00:00:19.508 --> 00:00:22.118
but these ingredients
and this spilling soup

00:00:22.118 --> 00:00:24.143
were all added with CG.

00:00:24.143 --> 00:00:27.040
Frozen-in-time sequences
have come a long way

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.060
thanks to advancements
in technology and CGI.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.070
Just look at this time-freeze
scene in "The Twilight Zone."

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.860
Here, boy. Come on!

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.643
Narrator: Without CG, it was difficult

00:00:36.643 --> 00:00:38.118
for director Douglas Hayes

00:00:38.118 --> 00:00:41.640
to hide subtle motions
like breathing and shaking.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.050
So he found that the best way

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.115
to cover up these unavoidable errors

00:00:45.115 --> 00:00:47.040
was to keep moving the camera,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.900
so viewers didn't even
have time to notice.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.080
The movement trick is still used today,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.510
but modern cameras also allow filmmakers

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.068
to slow down the visuals by
changing the frame rates.

00:00:58.068 --> 00:01:02.130
Most modern movies are shot
at 24 frames per second.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.690
If you increase the frame rate,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.310
more images are produced,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.200
allowing filmmakers to stretch out

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.312
and slow the scene down frame by frame.

00:01:10.312 --> 00:01:13.857
So, for example, this moment
in 1999's "The Matrix" ...

00:01:13.857 --> 00:01:15.095
Freeze it.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.986
Narrator: The camera was actually running

00:01:17.986 --> 00:01:20.494
at 96 frames per second.

00:01:20.494 --> 00:01:22.320
And to capture "Quicksilver time"

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.960
in "Days of Future Past" and "Apocalypse,"

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.360
the "X-Men" team used a Phantom camera

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.014
capable of both filming
3,000 frames per second

00:01:31.014 --> 00:01:33.690
and moving at 90 miles per hour.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.267
So an action that happened in
just one second in real time

00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:39.780
could be extended to 15 seconds.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.472
VFX artists can also
retime a live-action shot,

00:01:43.472 --> 00:01:45.780
a technique that was
required to make Quicksilver

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.744
look like he was moving
faster than his environment.

00:01:48.744 --> 00:01:50.490
That meant rotoscoping the actor

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.070
out of the original shot frame by frame,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.080
then filming him in
front of a green screen

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.060
so he could be retimed separately

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.186
before adding him back in.

00:01:59.186 --> 00:02:01.950
Filling these scenes with
various frozen characters

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.800
and background elements can
be done both practically

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.630
and with visual effects.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.743
And not everything in the
Quicksilver scenes was VFX.

00:02:09.743 --> 00:02:13.221
Just look at these dishes
propped up by wires and poles.

00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:16.380
Often practical effects in
combination with CG touch-ups

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.550
can add even more realism,

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.597
like in 2021's "The Worst
Person in the World,"

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.270
which leaned heavily into the
practical side for this scene,

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:31.020
in which the world freezes
around the protagonist, Julie.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.486
Director Joachim Trier
made a creative choice

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:37.210
to do most of the film's
frozen-in-time effects in-camera.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.900
Much of this was achieved
through the clever craftsmanship

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.970
of prop master Hedda Virik.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.420
Hedda: When it works, it works,

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.694
and it creates a magic on set

00:02:45.694 --> 00:02:48.590
that everyone is just very
excited to be a part of.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.130
Narrator: Like when Aksel freezes

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.020
in the middle of pouring coffee.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.072
To create this effect,

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:56.220
Hedda studied photos
of real coffee streams

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.280
and sculpted a replica using polymer clay.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.740
Hedda: It's not just one line of coffee.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:06.268
It's water, and water frozen
in time is always unique,

00:03:06.268 --> 00:03:09.570
and you get those little
beautiful lines down.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.580
Narrator: That sculpt
was used as the basis for

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.030
a negative cast, which she
poured resin into to add color.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:19.605
Hedda: So you really want
that see-through, lush coffee.

00:03:19.605 --> 00:03:21.480
Narrator: There was also
real coffee in the pot

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:22.890
to get the movement before

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.003
and after the frozen sequence.

00:03:25.003 --> 00:03:27.111
So to keep the real coffee from spilling,

00:03:27.111 --> 00:03:30.000
Hedda covered the pot's
opening with ultraclear tape.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.207
And the resin stream was
rigged to a coffee cup

00:03:32.207 --> 00:03:35.130
with a flexible wire so it
could be repositioned on set.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.930
While items like the coffee stream

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.961
needed to be made from scratch,

00:03:38.961 --> 00:03:41.730
others just needed some
clever modifications

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.128
to appear frozen.

00:03:43.128 --> 00:03:45.300
Like these headphones, which were taped

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.773
to this extra's shirt
to maintain their shape.

00:03:47.773 --> 00:03:49.590
Hedda: Sometimes the real thing works,

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.840
as long as there's no wind.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.060
Narrator: Similarly, Hedda
used tape and metal wire

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.802
on a real briefcase so it wouldn't flap.

00:03:56.802 --> 00:03:58.860
Inanimate objects are one challenge,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.420
but keeping everything in place

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.310
gets even more difficult
when you're dealing

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.440
with living, breathing creatures.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.013
Hedda: Animals are very
difficult to convince

00:04:08.013 --> 00:04:10.509
and to get them to do what you want.

00:04:10.509 --> 00:04:12.875
And I don't know any animal

00:04:12.875 --> 00:04:16.996
that just wants to stand
still and do nothing.

00:04:16.996 --> 00:04:18.720
Narrator: So these two
dogs in the background

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.490
are completely fake.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.260
But the fake pups needed to be placed

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.000
in unique and dynamic ways,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.430
so they wouldn't just
look like stuffed animals.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.100
That included breaking
one of the stuffed legs.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.890
Hedda: Like he's doing a little strut.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.750
Narrator: And holding
them down with rocks,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.370
so they wouldn't fall over.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:38.218
Human actors can stand still on command,

00:04:38.218 --> 00:04:41.667
but maintaining certain positions
for long periods of time

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:43.770
still requires some extra assistance

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.840
and can be painful and uncomfortable.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.350
And we were at each location
for hours and hours.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.041
When you're doing it again
and again and again and again,

00:04:52.041 --> 00:04:53.338
then you're going to get tired.

00:04:53.338 --> 00:04:54.720
Narrator: Wires are one option,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.430
like in this shot from "Apocalypse."

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.290
But they have their own complications.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:04:59.940
Ian: If you use wires all the time,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.440
sometimes you'll have sort of pinching

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.010
or an unnatural kind of angling.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.560
Narrator: So how did Hedda
get everyone to look so steady

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.051
in this final shot without wires?

00:05:10.051 --> 00:05:12.540
Tiny wooden blocks.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.080
She brought blocks of
all sizes to the set.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.825
The ones she chose varied
based on the type of footwear.

00:05:18.825 --> 00:05:22.336
On their own, the blocks
would be really easy to spot,

00:05:22.336 --> 00:05:25.650
so Hedda covered them in
nonreflective black tape.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.630
That way, they could
blend in to the shadow

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.071
under an extra's foot.

00:05:30.071 --> 00:05:32.190
The best option is to cover up these rigs

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.763
with people and objects.

00:05:33.763 --> 00:05:36.090
This moped simply has its kickstand out,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.097
but it was blocked from the camera.

00:05:38.097 --> 00:05:40.980
Bikes and scooters were a bit
more difficult to position,

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.384
as their kickstands are harder to hide.

00:05:43.384 --> 00:05:45.600
So Hedda built specialized metal stands

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:46.800
to hold them up straight

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.660
to appear as if they were mid-ride.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.170
Hedda: We had to position
something under one of the pedals

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.600
so that all of the weight
could be on the pedal.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.610
Narrator: Hedda also
had to teach the extras

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.020
how to position their bodies

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.970
for the scene to look realistic.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.550
Foremost, don't shift your weight.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:03.672
Hedda: Because when you're on a bike,

00:06:03.672 --> 00:06:05.308
you just want to go, right?

00:06:05.308 --> 00:06:06.540
You are used to being on a bike,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.068
and you treat it completely differently

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:11.280
to when you have to fake being on a bike.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.740
Narrator: Even when you use
practical for these sequences,

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.980
a final VFX assist is crucial
to put it all together

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.870
to make it look seamless.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:20.730
The bike and foot rigs, for example,

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.062
were removed digitally in post.

00:06:23.062 --> 00:06:24.540
The same goes for those wires

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.734
and poles in Quicksilver's scenes.

00:06:26.734 --> 00:06:29.645
While animators can
stabilize real performances

00:06:29.645 --> 00:06:31.980
to get rid of any moving and shaking,

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.021
digital doubles give
them much more control.

00:06:35.021 --> 00:06:37.329
So to get every detail just right,

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:39.712
"Days of Future Past" and
"Apocalypse" recruited

00:06:39.712 --> 00:06:42.880
the visual effects studio
Rising Sun Pictures.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.910
For instance, Rising Sun Pictures

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:48.120
created digital versions of
these students Quicksilver saves

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.620
to preserve the delicate balance

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.140
of keeping these characters frozen in time

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.505
but not frozen in place.

00:06:54.505 --> 00:06:56.749
That's also why they created a CG version

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:59.100
of this table for Quicksilver to surf on.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.140
We can use that to interact

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.133
with any effects that we're creating.

00:07:03.133 --> 00:07:04.470
Narrator: Like here, when the animators

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.778
rebuilt a car digitally so they could add

00:07:06.778 --> 00:07:09.330
slo-mo water splashing onto the tires.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:10.470
You can drive a car through,

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.930
and that would work in the final shot.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.667
But it's the splash that you
needed to sort of slow down

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:17.880
that you might not
necessarily get correct.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.520
Because once you film something
with that element in it,

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:21.840
that's what you've got.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.490
Narrator: Techniques
like facial replacement

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.400
can fix continuity errors.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.983
As time slows down in
"Days of Future Past,"

00:07:27.983 --> 00:07:29.664
Professor X yells ...

00:07:29.664 --> 00:07:31.113
No!

00:07:32.179 --> 00:07:34.710
Ian: When they actually filmed
it, his mouth was closed,

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:39.456
but contextually, he would've
been more into his word.

00:07:39.456 --> 00:07:41.280
Narrator: So his original
expression was swapped

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.710
with one where his mouth was wide open.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:45.180
In any one of these sequences,

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.100
there's the moment when time resumes

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.044
and everything goes back to normal.

00:07:50.044 --> 00:07:52.320
Since "Worst Person"'s
frozen-in-time sequence

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.900
ended back with Aksel as he poured coffee,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.900
they only had to make a single
action return to normal.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.210
But for "Days of Future
Past," they had to sort out

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.430
all the objects Magneto
lifted into the air

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.920
alongside whatever positions Quicksilver

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.916
placed all these characters in.

00:08:06.916 --> 00:08:09.300
On set, people and
objects were tossed around

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.270
using wires and pressurized air.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:13.247
If that scene works and the timing works

00:08:13.247 --> 00:08:15.750
as we get back into normal time,

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:18.150
then we'll try and keep as much as we can.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.220
Narrator: But only the
objects within reason.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.743
All these bits of leftover
food and extra plates

00:08:22.743 --> 00:08:26.000
that land on the ground in
the kitchen and keep moving?

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:26.833
Fake.

00:08:26.833 --> 00:08:28.440
Ian: Pizza dog, who ended up becoming

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.000
a favorite on our crew.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.374
So, there was a journey
through the mansion

00:08:33.374 --> 00:08:35.496
that was filmed with the cameras.

00:08:35.496 --> 00:08:38.070
So there was pizza dog with
his jowls sort of flapping,

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:39.480
which was a CG head.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.730
And here is some slow-moving footage

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:44.090
where we've taken various takes.

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.010
And so we would mush that journey behind

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:50.160
with the motion blur and the
speed so that it feels like,

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:51.672
"Oh, this is what it must be like."

00:08:51.672 --> 00:08:52.505
Quicksilver: Wow!

